K=[
('Channel mechanism:','Light-gated cation and anion channels alter conductance directly. The ionic gradient and membrane voltage determine the electrical consequence; actuator expression alone does not establish excitation or inhibition.'),
('Sufficiency and necessity:','Optical activation tests whether a targeted population can recruit an action. Ablation or verified silencing tests its contribution to normal performance. Neither intervention automatically identifies the complete natural circuit.'),
('State dependence:','The behavioral effect of neuronal activation depends on stimulation strength, preceding behavior and social history. Identical optical input need not produce an invariant action.'),
('Measurement validity:','Fluorescence requires motion and reference-channel controls. Wireless electrophysiology requires signal-fidelity and timing validation. Neither measurement is a direct causal test of behavioral function.'),
('Comparative evidence:','Annelid startle, zebrafish hunting, fly courtship and bat flight link distinct circuit arrangements to species-specific behavior. Connectomes and activity patterns constrain mechanisms without proving every functional connection.'),
('Behavioral controls:','Light-only and expression controls, anatomical verification, thermal calibration and preserved comparison behaviors limit alternative explanations. Wireless access expands behavior without removing preparation-specific constraints.')]
